# API Service Conventions

## Find the wrapper and a sibling service

Most codebases send HTTP through one wrapper service (base URL, headers, envelope and error handling) and give each backend domain an API service built on it. Find both before writing a method:

```bash
rg -l "HttpClient" apps libs src --glob '*.ts' --glob '!*.spec.ts'
```

The wrapper is the shared class that injects `HttpClient`; domain API services inject the wrapper. Read the wrapper's method signatures, a sibling domain service, and the backend controller action the method will call: HTTP verb, route, query/body shape, DTO property casing, and how the wrapper resolves the base URL. When a contract's placement, shape or exports change, settle that first with rule `client-models`, then write the service.

## HTTP wrapper and service structure

All feature HTTP calls go through a domain API service built on the wrapper. Never inject `HttpClient` in a component, effect or feature service, and never bypass the wrapper in a new API service. If the codebase has no wrapper, domain API services inject `HttpClient` themselves and are still the only HTTP callers; do not introduce a wrapper as a side effect.

New services use `@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })`, not module-scoped providers or `providedIn: 'any'`.

```typescript
@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })
export class WidgetApiService {
  private api = inject(ApiService);
  private endpoint = 'Widget';

  getWidgets(searchTerm: string): Observable<WidgetSummary[]> {
    return this.api.get<WidgetSummary[]>(`${this.endpoint}/GetWidgets`, { params: { searchTerm } });
  }

  saveWidget(request: SaveWidgetRequest): Observable<SaveWidgetResponse> {
    return this.api.post<SaveWidgetResponse>(`${this.endpoint}/SaveWidget`, request);
  }
}
```

`ApiService` stands for the codebase's wrapper. Use its actual signatures when passing body, options or mappers; a GET's second argument is usually options, not a separate action name.

Never inline named request/response interfaces in a service. Import wire contracts through the alias that rule `client-models` selects. Do not move contracts or add speculative alias migrations to a service change.

## Endpoint and HTTP method shapes

Build each URL from the backend's actual route attributes and the sibling methods, not from a naming convention. An ASP.NET `[controller]` route token is the class name without its `Controller` suffix (`WidgetController` -> `Widget`). Check whether the wrapper adds a prefix such as `/api/` or chooses among several configured base URLs, and do not assume every resolved URL has the same prefix. OData action routes can take the form `Widget(<id>)/Default.<Action>`. Do not replace an established route with the conventional pattern.

## Backend response normalization

Inspect the real response and the declared consumer shape. Read what the wrapper does by default: some unwrap an envelope such as OData's `value`; few convert PascalCase to camelCase. If a camelCase model needs a PascalCase response mapped, normalize at the API service boundary, through the wrapper's mapping argument if it has one or with `map` in the service, not in effects, reducers, selectors or components. Preserve actual wire casing when no conversion is needed.

```typescript
getWidgetList(): Observable<WidgetList> {
  return this.api.get<WidgetListDto>(`${this.endpoint}/GetWidgetList`).pipe(
    map((response) => ({
      items: (response.Items ?? []).map((item) => ({ itemId: item.ItemId, canOpen: item.CanOpen })),
    }))
  );
}
```

A mapper that replaces the wrapper's default envelope extraction must handle the envelope itself. Do not assume arbitrary DTO fields are camelCase or copy `Items ?? []` as a default for a required/non-list response.

## Service exports and verification

Export a new service from its library's public `index.ts` the way sibling services are exported, so consumers import it through the workspace alias (`tsconfig.base.json` `paths`) rather than a deep relative path.

Whether the service gets its own spec: `{CURSOR}/skills/unit-test-guidance/client-conventions.md` (spec creation boundary). Narrow model or service wiring changes use TypeScript and build verification, and model or import-boundary changes need the consuming app build. Commands and evidence: rule `client-build-loops`.
